title The gates that stand, and the one word actually worth asking for
prompt Taking inspiration from our outer and inner recursion loops, do you need my word about anything?
think The question is the recursion prompt's own stop rule asked out loud -- the loop is told to recur through all agent-doable work and print GATES-ONLY when nothing but custody gates remain. So the honest answer is a measurement, not an opinion: read the ledger, the card's gates, and the printed queue, and say which of the three states the tree is actually in.
think Read the reds ledger first, as Standfast asks. REDS %103 -- the card that named the round before it, because the push came before the pin -- closed at 20260821.020803, repaired by doing rather than by claiming: fold M's send staged the card inside the work commit. No live red stands. The corrective queue is empty, so the constructive one governs.
think Read the constructive queue next, and it is not short. The fourth meter prints it largest-first every lap: reckon_the_plan at 2,700 carried across 37 rungs, then fill_table, provision_notes, confer_one, weigh_the_term, and six more -- 703 families carrying 137,140 lines. Every one of them is design, code, and witness work, which the card names agent-doable in plain words. Not one of them touches a gate.
think Read the six custody gates and check each against what is actually queued. Seed force-push, provisioning and paying, funds and keys, Keaton's own Kumara instance, deep debride, a collaborator's domain. Nothing in the fold arc reaches any of them. So the loop would not print GATES-ONLY; it would keep folding.
think That makes the true answer no -- and stopping there would be the cheap answer. A loop that only ever reports its stop rule never surfaces the choices a hand would want. Two things genuinely want a word, and neither blocks the next lap.
obs The first is a direction call rather than a gate. Lindy-first orders the queue by what still reads true on the long return, and the fold arc is carry-reduction: real, compounding, and nine laps deep with checks and spine unmoved. Yet the card names Kumara SLH-DSA-SHAKE-256s as seated and its library as agent-doable, and Aurora's eight boot stages already cross-build. Ten more folds and a crypto floor are both honest uses of the same hours. The queue outlasts many laps either way, so nothing is lost by choosing.
obs The second is a debride, which by law wants an explicit word naming its target. crux/REMEMBER.md stands near 200 KB, over its own advisory bound, and the card itself booked that as a condensation debt for a later lap. Condensing a live card is exactly the act the cairn rule exists to precede. I would not open it without the word, and I did not.
obs Named a third thing and then set it down: the fourth meter compares exact text, so a nearly-alike body stays invisible until a hand looks -- fold M found the first instance. Widening the meter to see near-twins is ordinary agent-doable design work, so it wants no word at all, only a lap.
obs Wrote nothing to the tree beyond this log. A counsel turn that answers a question is not a work round, and the reds-first ledger, the gates, and the queue all read the same after it as before.
file session-logs/20260821-021754_the-gates-that-stand-and-the-one-word-worth-asking.kyri the reading itself -- ledger clean, gates untouched, queue long
recommend keep-going No gate blocks the next lap: fold N on reckon_the_plan, 2,700 carried across 37 rungs, reach verified pub before a line moves. The two words worth having -- fold arc versus the Kumara crypto floor, and whether to condense the operator card -- are offers, not blocks.
